\section{\textbf{\Large Python Programming}}

In this chapter we will study the data types, features, and operators of Python programming.

\subsection{Introduction}

Python is a widely used programming language that offers several unique features and advantages compared to languages like Java and C++. In the late 1980s, Guido van Rossum dreamed of developing Python.

\subsubsection{Data Types}

\textbf{Numeric Datatype:}
In Python, numeric data type is used to hold numeric values. Integers, floating-point numbers, and complex numbers fall under Python numbers category. They are defined as \texttt{int}, \texttt{float}, and \texttt{complex}.

\textbf{Sequence Datatype:}
List is an ordered collection of items separated by commas and enclosed within brackets. Tuple is an ordered sequence of items similar to a list. The only difference is that tuples are immutable.

\subsubsection{Features of Python Programming}

\textbf{Easy to Use and Read:}
Python's syntax is clear and easy to read, making it an ideal language for beginners and experienced programmers.

\textbf{Cross Platform Compatibility:}
Python can be installed on Windows, macOS, and Linux, allowing developers to create software that runs across different operating systems.

\textbf{Free and Open Source:}
Python is freely available and open source. Its source code is also available to the public.

\textbf{Portable Language:}
Python is portable and can run on different platforms such as Windows, Linux, Unix, and macOS.

\subsubsection{Operators}

An operator is a symbol that performs a specific operation between two operands. Python has several types of operators.

\textbf{Arithmetic Operators:}
Arithmetic operators include \texttt{+}, \texttt{-}, \texttt{*}, \texttt{/}, \texttt{\%}, \texttt{**}, and \texttt{//}.

\textbf{Comparison Operators:}
Comparison operators compare two values and return a Boolean value. Examples are \texttt{==}, \texttt{!=}, \texttt{>}, \texttt{<}, \texttt{>=}, and \texttt{<=}.

\textbf{Assignment Operators:}
Assignment operators assign values to variables. Examples are \texttt{=}, \texttt{+=}, \texttt{-=}, \texttt{*=}, \texttt{\%=}, and \texttt{**=}.

\textbf{Bitwise Operators:}
Bitwise operators operate on bits. Examples are AND, OR, XOR, NOT, left shift, and right shift.

\textbf{Logical Operators:}
Logical operators are \texttt{and}, \texttt{or}, and \texttt{not}.

