\section{Introduction}

In this section we are discussing how to insert an image in a LaTeX document.

\subsection{Package}

LaTeX cannot manage images by itself. So we need to use the
\texttt{graphicx} package. To use it, we include the following line
in the preamble:

\texttt{\textbackslash usepackage\{graphicx\}}

The command
\texttt{\textbackslash graphicspath\{\{images/\}\}}
tells LaTeX that the images are kept in a folder named
\texttt{images} under the directory of the main document.

The \texttt{\textbackslash includegraphics\{universe\}} command is
the one that actually includes the image. The name of the file
containing the image is written without the extension. For example,
\texttt{universe.png} can be included as \texttt{universe}.

The image file name should not contain white spaces or multiple dots.
It also provides a way to change the width, height, and position of
the image.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{image}
    \caption{TeX Studio}
    \label{fig:enter-label}
\end{figure}

