\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[chapter]

\begin{document}

\chapter{Introduction}
\label{chap:introduction}

LaTeX is a document preparation system that is widely used for writing scientific,
mathematical, and technical documents. It allows users to create well-formatted
documents with equations, tables, figures, references, and bibliographies.

In this document, we demonstrate how to reference chapters, sections, footnotes,
and mathematical environments. The current chapter is
Chapter~\ref{chap:introduction}.

\section{Importance of LaTeX}
\label{sec:importance}

LaTeX is useful because it automatically numbers chapters, sections, equations,
and other environments. If the document is changed later, the numbers are
updated automatically.

For example, this is Section~\ref{sec:importance}. We can use the
\verb|\label| command to give a name to an item and the
\verb|\ref| command to refer to its number.

LaTeX is especially useful for mathematical documents because mathematical
expressions can be written clearly. For example,

\begin{equation}
a^2 + b^2 = c^2
\label{eq:pythagoras}
\end{equation}

is displayed as a mathematical equation.

\newpage

\section{Footnotes}
\label{sec:footnotes}

Footnotes can be added easily using the \verb|\footnote| command. For example,

LaTeX is widely used in universities and research institutions.
\footnote{LaTeX was originally developed from the TeX typesetting system created by Donald Knuth.}

The footnote appears automatically at the bottom of the page. There is no need
to manually number the footnote.

\section{Theorem Environment}
\label{sec:theorem}

We can create numbered environments such as theorems. The following is a simple
example.

\begin{theorem}
If two numbers are equal, then their squares are also equal.
\label{thm:squares}
\end{theorem}

The above statement is Theorem~\ref{thm:squares}. Thus, we can refer to an
environment by using its label.

\section{Cross Referencing}
\label{sec:crossref}

Cross-referencing makes a document easier to maintain. For example,
Chapter~\ref{chap:introduction} introduces LaTeX, while
Section~\ref{sec:importance} explains its importance. The mathematical result
is given in Theorem~\ref{thm:squares}.

The \texttt{hyperref} package makes these references clickable. When the PDF is
opened, clicking on a chapter, section, or theorem reference takes the reader
to the corresponding location.

\end{document}

